One manifest per userspace driver, dropped in rather than listed anywhere:
adding a driver should be adding a file.

The ones for devices on the PCI bus are **generated**, from what each driver
says it answers for in src/lib/driver.zig, by `zig build driver-manifests`.
The boot probe reads the same declaration, so a listing cannot say nobody
drives what something is driving, or the reverse. Change the declaration and
run the step; do not edit a generated file.

The ones for devices on the USB bus are written by hand: they are matched by
what a device says it is rather than by who made it, and the kernel's probe
never sees them, so there is nothing for them to disagree with.

Keys are the field names of `Manifest` in src/user/devmgd/main.zig, so this
directory and the parser cannot drift.

  name    what the driver is called, in logs and in the process list
  binary  absolute path to the program
  match   pci:VENDOR:DEVICE for one part, in hex
          pci-class:CLASS:SUBCLASS for a whole family
          An exact part always wins over a family.
  caps    comma-separated capabilities. Defaults to `driver`, which is what a
          driver needs and the most it should have: interrupt lines, I/O ports
          and device apertures, and nothing else.

A device the kernel already drives is left alone. Two drivers on one device is
worse than the wrong one of them.

`driver rescan` reads this directory again and binds what it finds, so a
driver added to a running machine comes up without a reboot. It walks the bus
first, so it also finds hardware that was not on it at boot: a part switched
on with `hw` announces itself to nothing.
